**The SOQL-in-a-loop in `PricingRules.standardDiscountFor`.**

`PricingRules.standardDiscountFor(Id)` runs a fresh `SELECT … FROM Product2 WHERE Id = :productId` on every invocation. When the CPQ tool inserts 200 lines in one transaction, the trigger fires 200 calls → 200 SOQL statements → **Limit Exceeded: SOQL queries** (limit 100/tx). The same problem would appear on `before update` or `before undelete` if you ever extend the trigger.

**Recommended fix (requires a one-line addition to `PricingRules`, not a rewrite):**

Add a bulk overload so the caller can resolve many products in a single query:

```apex
/** Standard discount (percent) for a set of product Ids – one query. */
public static Map<Id, Decimal> standardDiscountFor(Set<Id> productIds) {
    Map<Id, Decimal> discounts = new Map<Id, Decimal>();
    if (productIds == null || productIds.isEmpty()) return discounts;

    List<Product2> products = [
        SELECT Id, Family FROM Product2 WHERE Id IN :productIds
    ];
    for (Product2 p : products) {
        discounts.put(p.Id, standardDiscountForFamily(p.Family));
    }
    return discounts;
}
```

The existing single-Id method can stay (delegates to the map or keeps its query for ad-hoc / FLS use), so no existing caller breaks. The handler then becomes:

```apex
public static void handleBeforeInsert() {
    // 1. Collect the Ids we actually need to look up
    Set<Id> productIds = new Set<Id>();
    for (OpportunityLineItem oli : Trigger.new) {
        if (oli.Discount == null && oli.Product2Id != null) {
            productIds.add(oli.Product2Id);
        }
    }
    if (productIds.isEmpty()) return;

    // 2. ONE query for all of them
    Map<Id, Decimal> discounts = PricingRules.standardDiscountFor(productIds);

    // 3. Apply
    for (OpportunityLineItem oli : Trigger.new) {
        if (oli.Discount == null && oli.Product2Id != null) {
            oli.Discount = discounts.get(oli.Product2Id);   // null-safe: absent key → null → line stays null
        }
    }
}
```

